      {
        "id": 55770,
        "name": "TheSoundOfTrees",
        "username": "TheSoundOfTrees",
        "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/t/da6949/{size}.png",
        "created_at": "2013-03-22T13:26:15.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"j1hopki1\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/j/58f4c7/48.png\" class=\"avatar\"> j1hopki1:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>SoundofTrees, I really&nbsp;don't think Legacy is a difficulty zero. As a support hero you make the best plays when you know the other heroes' decks hands and abilities.&nbsp;I wouldn't move him to a two, but playing Legacy is not always easy. I would argue that Ra is easier to grasp. Playing Ra can sometimes feel like asking yourself \"Should I do lots of&nbsp;damage or should I do a lot damage?\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>True, Legacy becomes better with experience. But when you begin to play, as most of his cards have indirect effect by boosting/protecting the team, you can play nearly anything and have everyone else become more effective. You can have big effects on the game without even knowing why <img src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/images/emoji/apple/slight_smile.png?v=12\" title=\":slight_smile:\" class=\"emoji\" alt=\":slight_smile:\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\"> \"dificulty Zero\" is an exageration, of course - but he probably is the hero that feels the more effective as support, even if you don't know what you're doing.</p>",
        "post_number": 27,
        "post_type": 1,
